.\" Conforms to POSIX.1-2017
.TH YACC_GRAMMAR 5 "2026-04-02" "IEEE/The Open Group" "POSIX Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
yacc_grammar \- yacc grammar file format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
%{
  \fI/* C declarations */\fP
%}
\fI/* yacc declarations */\fP
%%
\fI/* grammar rules */\fP
%%
\fI/* user C code */\fP
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A grammar file for the
.BR yacc (1)
compiler generator consists of three sections separated by lines containing only the double percent symbol
.RB ( %% ).
The first section is the declarations section, the second is the rules section, and the third is the user code section.
.PP
The third section is optional, and if it is omitted, the second
.B %%
separator may also be omitted.
.SH DECLARATIONS SECTION
The declarations section defines the tokens and nonterminal symbols used in the grammar, establishes their associative precedence, and allows the inclusion of arbitrary C code.
.PP
C code to be inserted at the beginning of the generated parser is enclosed within
.B %{
and
.BR %} .
This area is typically used to include headers and declare function prototypes (such as
.BR yylex ()
and
.BR yyerror ()).
.PP
Tokens and associativity are declared using predefined macros:
.TP
.B %token \fIname\fP \fI...\fP
Declares terminal symbols (tokens). Numeric values can be explicitly assigned using
.B %token \fIname\fP \fIvalue\fP.
.TP
.B %left \fIname\fP \fI...\fP
Declares a left-associative token, such as subtraction
.RB ( \- ),
used for precedence resolution.
.TP
.B %right \fIname\fP \fI...\fP
Declares a right-associative token, such as an assignment operator
.RB ( = ).
.TP
.B %nonassoc \fIname\fP \fI...\fP
Declares a token that cannot be used associatively (for example, relational operators).
.TP
.B %union { \fItype_declarations\fP }
Specifies the exact C structure of the
.B YYSTYPE
union used for semantic values.
.TP
.B %type <\fItag\fP> \fIname\fP \fI...\fP
Assigns the specific union member
.I tag
to the listed nonterminal or token names to enable strong typing of semantic values.
.TP
.B %start \fIsymbol\fP
Declares the root nonterminal of the grammar. If omitted, the left-hand side of the first rule is used.
.SH RULES SECTION
The rules section contains the production rules defining the context-free grammar. A rule consists of a left-hand side nonterminal followed by a colon
.RB ( : ),
and one or more right-hand side productions separated by vertical bars
.RB ( | ).
The rule is terminated with a semicolon
.RB ( ; ).
.PP
.nf
nonterminal : right_hand_side_1
            | right_hand_side_2
            ;
.fi
.PP
Right-hand sides may contain terminal symbols (tokens), nonterminal symbols, character literals
.RB ( 'c' ),
and blocks of C code enclosed in braces
.RB ( { " ... " } )
representing the semantic actions to be executed when the production is reduced.
.SH ACTIONS
Within a semantic action block, the semantic value of the left-hand side nonterminal is denoted by
.BR $$ .
The semantic values of the right-hand side symbols are denoted by
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
and so on, corresponding to their order of appearance in the rule.
.PP
If the semantic values are typed via
.BR %union ,
their members can be accessed directly using
.BI $< tag >$
and
.BI $< tag >n\fR.
.PP
The default action for an empty rule is to set
.B $$
to an undefined state. The default action for a rule with elements is:
.nf
    { $$ = $1; }
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
%token NUMBER
%left '+' '\-'
%%
expr : expr '+' expr { $$ = $1 + $3; }
     | expr '\-' expr { $$ = $1 \- $3; }
     | NUMBER        { $$ = $1; }
     ;
%%
int yylex(void) { /* ... */ }
void yyerror(const char *s) { /* ... */ }
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yacc (1),
.BR lex (1)
